Z||000024||Wolfhound||12-16-1999||05:42 PM||||You can tow a 4 or 5 speed OK and you can tow a automatic on a dolly if you put the rear wheels on . Make sure your sunroof hood and deck are secure. If any of these open while towing, their gone.||||reg||14
Z||000025||Monkeyman||12-16-1999||05:55 PM||||I'm always looking for Fieros.  Now that I have mine, I'll keep my eye out for great deals for everyone else.  Thanx for all the help.||||reg||1
Z||000026||DRH||12-16-1999||05:58 PM||||Monkeyman,<P>Looks like you got a great deal. Should be worth the wait. <P>On older front engine, RWD cars with an automatic I've always heard you shouldn't tow them with the rear wheels on the ground, unless you pull the drive shaft. I believe it's because the transmission bearings don't get lubricated properly without the fluid being circulated. Not sure if this applies to RWD transaxles (I guess the Fiero would be almost alone in that catagory). Maybe some of the real Fiero mechanics can help here.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000027||william_thorniii||12-16-1999||06:34 PM||||To make a general rule of thumb.... always tow Fiero's with the rear wheels on the dolly.  The same applies if being towed by truck.  The reasoning is that on automatic transmission equipt vehicles, the fluid is not pumped when in neutral thereby not allowing the transmission to dissipate heat.  This heat buildup can cause severe problems.  The problem.... U-Haul claims that Fiero's cannot be towed on a dolly.  It can be done, but there is dangerously little clearance.  <P>To tow a Fiero on a dolly follow these steps:<BR>1) Straighten the front wheels and lock the steering wheel.  <BR>2) Wrap the seatbelt around the steering wheel.  This is only a precautionary measure in case the wheel lock breaks... you have a backup.<BR>3) Firmly set the parking brake and put in gear or park.<BR>4) Ensure the sunroof latches properly,  otherwise remove it<BR>5) Ensure that the front compartment lid is latched well.  <BR>6) Double and triple check the straps and hitch.<BR>7) Put it in gear and you are in business.  <P>Good luck Monkeyman... P.S. I towed my 85 back from Hendersonville, KY with this procedure.  Everything worked well.  <P><BR>||||reg||3
Z||000028||Monkeyman||12-17-1999||01:27 AM||||Thanx, again, William.  I wondered about the clearance at the front if it was put on a dolly backwards.  I'd better call U-Haul tomorrow and see what the price difference is between a dolly and a trailer.  If there's not a lot of difference, it might be worth it for me to pay the guy the difference between the 2.||||reg||1
Z||000029||DJRice||12-17-1999||09:02 AM||||Umm. A bit of experience with the UHAUL trailers.  I hope you are not a very big guy, because I used one and couldnt get the door open enough (because it was so low) to get out of the car.  My point is that if you do this, kinda make sure that you get the car far enough over on the trailer to facilitate opening the drivers door with it on the trailer.||||reg||1
Z||000030||RossT||12-17-1999||09:42 AM||||When I bought my fiero, I rented a standard<BR>drive your car onto it trailer (not dolly) from u-haul.<BR>On level ground there was no clearance problems. The trailor is kind of heavy, thus you would need a full size car or truck to tow it. I have pictures of my 88gt on the trailor that show the clearance. I can e-mail them to you if you want to see. The part<BR>over the trailer wheels folds down so you can<BR>easily open the door and step out after you drive the fiero on to it.<P>Ross||||reg||1
Z||000031||RossT||12-17-1999||12:55 PM||||I posted pics of what a Fiero gt looks like on a standard u-haul drive on trailor under the thread "Don't you wish you were buying a Gt". disregard the dude with the big smile!!||||reg||1
Z||000032||Monkeyman||12-17-1999||01:50 PM||||Thanx for the advice.  I called U-Haul early this am and they said that they have to match the vehicle being towed and the towing vehicle with the trailer.  They said that the only way they would allow a trailer to be rented for a Fiero GT is if I get a special override code from someone.  I guessed that there wasn't much clearance getting the car on or off.  Also, they want $70 (!) for a one way trip from Chicago area to Wabash, IN!!  Damn!  I may have to look into alternative transportation.  The seller said that he would bring it here, but he was probably counting on spending less and using a dolly.  I'd rather not use a dolly unless another Fieroholic is doing the transporting.  I want to make sure I don't have to replace the tranny or a front fascia!||||reg||1
Z||000033||RossT||12-17-1999||02:30 PM||||They gave me the same story about the clearance. It really was not a problem. The total weight of the car and trailer together<BR>is very close to 5000lbs. They should alow any v-8 to tow that.||||reg||1
